WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.770
- Is there also differences by

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:05.415
individuals who prefer morning, night,

00:00:05.415 --> 00:00:08.400
and then maybe as well, in terms

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.590
of not just the hours of sleep,

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:12.750
but the time of the
day in which you sleep?

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:14.636
- This comes back to a concept

00:00:14.636 --> 00:00:18.690
biologically well-defined,
called your chronotype.

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.240
So are you a morning type, evening type

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:22.590
or somewhere in between?

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:23.423
Depending on the data,

00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:27.150
it's about a third/third
split across the population.

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:31.050
I am, unfortunately, just
like the rest of my pedantry,

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.840
in terms of personality,
I'm desperately boring.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.250
I am completely vanilla.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:39.153
I'm a neutral, I'm sort of
like an 11 to 7 kind of guy.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:44.288
But your chronotype as to when you want

00:00:44.288 --> 00:00:46.040
to go to bed and wake up,

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.970
it's not your fault, and
it's not your choice.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:53.730
Because it turns out that
it's genetically determined,

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:57.720
and we now know that there
are at least 9 different genes

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:02.720
that will dictate your
morningness versus eveningness

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.340
preference, in terms of being a sleeper,

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.910
what we call, are you a night
owl or are you a morning lark?

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:11.100
It's gifted to you at birth.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.030
It's hardwired and it's
very difficult to to change.

00:01:15.030 --> 00:01:18.180
And by the way, if you want to
know truly what type you are,

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:19.710
you can go online and you can search

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:22.770
for something called the MEQ, which stands

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.320
for the morningness eveningness

00:01:25.622 --> 00:01:27.600
questionnaire for chronotype.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.600
And it takes about 3 or
4 minutes, really easy,

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:31.953
and you get your type.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:34.830
You can also do it in a quick fashion.

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:36.430
You can just say, look, you know

00:01:38.013 --> 00:01:40.722
if you or I were on a desert
island, nothing to wake up for,

00:01:40.722 --> 00:01:44.130
we could go to bed whenever
we really wanted to,

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:46.590
and whenever we really desired to wake up

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:48.660
rather than when society forces us

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:52.410
to go to bed and wake up,
what time would that be?

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.360
And for most people, that time

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.090
as to when they would like to go to bed

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.520
and like to wake up is very different

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.340
than what time they actually are currently

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:05.490
trying to go to bed and trying to wake up,

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:10.170
which suggests that modernity
has inserted a wedge

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:13.740
of a mismatch in terms of our sleep timing

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.950
based on our chronotype, because society

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.590
is desperately geared towards and biased

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.420
towards morning types.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:24.900
You know, it's this, the
early bird catches the worm.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.670
Those are the people that are praised.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:30.100
That leaves out in the cold
and chastised wrongly so,

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.363
as if it's their choice,
the evening types.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:36.330
And the early bird may catch the worm.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:39.900
But I would also say that the
second mouse gets the cheese.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.450
And so, you know, there's, lots of ways

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.150
that we should be much
more kind to ourselves

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:48.510
and others in society,
regarding evening types.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:51.750
The practical part of it
comes to people that I'll see

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.256
at my sleep center here who will say,

00:02:54.256 --> 00:02:57.000
"Look, I just have terrible insomnia.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:02:58.890
I have difficulty falling asleep.

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:01.350
I go to bed and I'm just wide
awake for the first hour.

00:03:01.350 --> 00:03:03.150
I just cannot fall asleep."

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:04.710
And then I'll ask them a little bit more

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:06.570
about their chronotype.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:07.980
And it turns out that they're someone,

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.750
who at the weekend finally,

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:13.500
they would normally be going
to bed about midnight, 1:00 AM,

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:16.920
but during the week, because
of their schedule, of course,

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.810
and understandably so,
they're forcing themselves

00:03:20.779 --> 00:03:22.590
to get into bed at 10:00 PM,

00:03:22.590 --> 00:03:24.570
which is a 2 to 3 hour difference,

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:27.120
relative to your natural chronotype.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.840
So no wonder your biology
is not even yet ready

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.970
to try and help you fall asleep,

00:03:32.970 --> 00:03:36.540
but you are sitting there in
bed just wishing it to happen,

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.990
and you can't wish for
sleep to happen and make-

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:42.343
it's not like a light
switch in that sense.

00:03:42.343 --> 00:03:45.300
And so it masquerades
almost like what we call

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:47.760
a form of sleep-onset insomnia.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.490
There's also something called
sleep maintenance insomnia.

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:52.950
So do you have difficulties
falling asleep?

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.470
Or difficulties staying asleep?

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:57.630
And they're not mutually
exclusive, by the way.

00:03:57.630 --> 00:04:00.720
but people with a chronotype
who are night owls,

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:02.430
they can sometimes have that challenge.

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:06.360
They're in bed too early,
and they can't fall asleep.

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:09.480
- That's probably one of
the most important points

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.330
to discuss today, because
as a primary care physician,

00:04:12.330 --> 00:04:15.270
obviously insomnia, whether
it's the start of sleep

00:04:15.270 --> 00:04:17.640
or disrupted sleep, not
being able to fall asleep

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.509
afterwards, upon waking in
the middle of the night,

00:04:20.509 --> 00:04:24.884
it's easy for me as a
physician to either look

00:04:24.884 --> 00:04:29.220
just to lifestyle changes,
talk about sleep hygiene,

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:31.770
discuss caffeine usage, alcohol usage,

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:36.240
things that would impact
sleep from my training level,

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.640
and maybe even mental health.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.200
That would be a step further that I go

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.196
that most physicians
probably don't even get to

00:04:42.196 --> 00:04:45.000
in their 15 minute visits, unfortunately,

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:46.533
in our current healthcare system.

00:04:46.533 --> 00:04:47.640
- Yeah.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.980
- But should we be, as physicians,

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:52.590
thinking about chronotype more?

00:04:52.590 --> 00:04:54.900
- I think we should
certainly be asking that

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:59.460
as part of the equation and
being sensitive to that,

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:00.900
in terms of understanding it.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:02.820
It turns out that there's a whole field

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:07.230
of chronobiology and chronopharmacology

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:10.012
because as we've now
started to understand,

00:05:10.012 --> 00:05:13.727
drugs can have different
potencies of effect

00:05:13.727 --> 00:05:16.470
on the basis of different times

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:19.470
during the 24-hour cycle,
and there may be more

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:24.360
or less potent times for you
to provide that medication.

00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:26.970
You know, sometimes for
hypertensive medications

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:29.730
there's some evidence that
time of day is important.

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:31.320
There's a big field demonstrating

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:35.220
that cancer medication can
have a chronotype dependency,

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:38.460
non-trivial in terms of its efficacy.

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:40.086
So I think that that's important.

00:05:40.086 --> 00:05:43.080
I also, you know, I love
the way that you think about

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:47.258
not just going to sleep
hygiene, which is critical,

00:05:47.258 --> 00:05:50.580
and being mindful of alcohol,
which is no friend of sleep,

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:53.610
and caffeine equally so, and
then moving to mental health,

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:57.180
because what we know is that
perhaps one of the biggest

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:01.920
common central pathways through
which your body instigates

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:05.520
this thing called insomnia
is having an overactive

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:08.652
fight-or-flight stress
system within the body,

00:06:08.652 --> 00:06:12.480
which is part of your, the
automatic nervous system.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:14.430
It's called the
sympathetic nervous system.

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:15.900
It's anything but sympathetic.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:17.670
It's very agitating, aggravating.

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:19.680
It's the fight-or-flight branch,

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:22.860
Together with something
called the HPA axis,

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.020
which is part of your hormonal axis,

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:28.620
particularly regarding a
hormone called cortisol,

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:30.320
which is a stress-related hormone.

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:33.300
And when those things are switched on,

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:38.250
which sort of high levels of
anxiety, worry, and stress

00:06:38.250 --> 00:06:42.540
will very much do, sleep
is almost impossible.

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:44.160
And it results in people-

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:47.362
the phenomenon that I would
describe as tired but wired

00:06:47.362 --> 00:06:50.361
where they are so desperately tired,

00:06:50.361 --> 00:06:53.220
but they're just so, from a
mental health perspective,

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:55.200
to your point, they're just so wired

00:06:55.200 --> 00:06:56.970
that they can't fall asleep.

00:06:56.970 --> 00:06:59.340
And until you actually
target the treatment

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:01.590
of the mental health component,

00:07:01.590 --> 00:07:05.130
it's going to be very difficult to-

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:07.920
I mean, one could just
simply offer, you know,

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:11.760
sedative sleep agents to try
and knock the cortex out.

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:14.220
But we know that there are
increasingly better ways,

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:17.762
and I think one of the
things that physicians

00:07:17.762 --> 00:07:20.340
have maybe started doing is becoming aware

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:23.430
of the the current recommended alternative

00:07:23.430 --> 00:07:24.810
to sleeping pills, which
is something called

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:27.780
cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia,

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:30.240
or CBTI, for short.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:33.510
And I would say that for any sleep, sorry,

00:07:33.510 --> 00:07:36.630
for any general physician, any GP,

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:39.600
who is facing a patient who is saying

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:41.430
that they are struggling with sleep,

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:43.590
trying to at first direct them

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:47.280
towards cognitive behavioral
therapy for insomnia

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:49.080
and trying to find a provider for that.

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:50.610
I know it's difficult.

00:07:50.610 --> 00:07:52.290
It turns out, if you look
at the number of people

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:53.700
who can provide it,

00:07:53.700 --> 00:07:56.430
there is a mismatch
between demand and supply.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:58.740
There's not enough supply for the demand.

00:07:58.740 --> 00:08:03.000
Nevertheless, being aware of
cognitive behavioral therapy

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:04.620
for insomnia is critical.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:07.530
There are now some apps
that you can use as well

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:09.780
to help with that, to
make it more convenient.

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:12.322
That's the only thing I
would add to that equation.

00:08:12.322 --> 00:08:14.010
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:16.350
I'm a big proponent of CBT.

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:19.746
I even have been able to
institute some introductory,

00:08:19.746 --> 00:08:22.650
I wouldn't say they're
introductory sessions,

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:27.650
but maybe basic understandings
that I can pass along

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:30.780
to my patients until they're able to see

00:08:30.780 --> 00:08:33.464
a mental health provider
about what CBT is,

00:08:33.464 --> 00:08:34.297
- Amazing, gosh.
- How it works.

00:08:34.297 --> 00:08:36.883
Where we work on thoughts
as opposed to feelings.

00:08:36.883 --> 00:08:40.920
Because in reality the
mind-body connection

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:44.550
is so much connected that
we give it credit for

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:48.233
that patients oftentimes are ready to get

00:08:48.233 --> 00:08:50.430
a physical diagnosis
and are really surprised

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:52.680
or even shocked when
everything comes up normal.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:55.470
The imaging, the lab
tests, and it's only until

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:57.840
we get to their mental
health do we figure out

00:08:57.840 --> 00:08:59.220
that's where the problem is,

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:01.620
at least beginning and
then showing itself.

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:03.331
So sleep is definitely one of those.

00:09:03.331 --> 00:09:04.164
- That's so powerful.

00:09:04.164 --> 00:09:06.990
You know, to think that a
physician in this day and age,

00:09:06.990 --> 00:09:09.461
and I know, I'm not pointing any fingers,

00:09:09.461 --> 00:09:12.390
the job that you have is
so immensely difficult

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:15.927
within these compressed,
zip-file time periods.

00:09:15.927 --> 00:09:17.730
You're supposed to go through

00:09:17.730 --> 00:09:19.932
this complex biological
organism in front of you,

00:09:19.932 --> 00:09:22.680
quickly make, make a diagnosis,

00:09:22.680 --> 00:09:24.600
understand the treatment path outcome,

00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:26.070
prescribe and get a-

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:28.650
you know, so that someone like you

00:09:28.650 --> 00:09:31.560
is being already able to think

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:34.260
about cognitive behavioral therapy,

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:38.640
and then give the stepping
stone components to help them

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.158
on their journey as they start
to seek out that further.

00:09:41.158 --> 00:09:45.450
I mean, how much better
would the world be, you know

00:09:45.450 --> 00:09:47.400
if that was more common practice?

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:49.170
I can't imagine.

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:52.050
- I think it would improve
health outcomes significantly.

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:55.200
That's why I'm a big proponent
of increasing funding

00:09:55.200 --> 00:09:58.290
for primary care because the struggle

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:01.470
that a lot of physicians have
is those 15 minute visits

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:03.300
with patients that are unknown to them,

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:04.710
that they don't have a history with.

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:07.230
Like the urgent care model,
where a patient comes

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:09.390
in to see a doctor
they've never seen before,

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:11.640
and somehow in 15 minutes this physician's

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:13.690
supposed to create a
rapport with this patient,

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:16.278
learn about their
condition, their symptoms,

00:10:16.278 --> 00:10:20.447
potentially connected
to some sort of cause,

00:10:20.447 --> 00:10:22.560
create the treatment, make sure

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:23.613
that the patient
understands the treatment,

00:10:23.613 --> 00:10:25.110
that they can afford the treatment,

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:26.910
they have access to the treatment,

00:10:26.910 --> 00:10:29.910
and then document that entire
interaction in 15 minutes.

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:31.200
On what planet?

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:33.922
And then make sure the insurance
approves that treatment.

00:10:33.922 --> 00:10:34.755
(laughs)

00:10:34.755 --> 00:10:35.670
- Right. Right.

00:10:35.670 --> 00:10:39.595
You know, what are we, what
are we doing if our goal

00:10:39.595 --> 00:10:42.430
is to try to care for other human beings

00:10:42.430 --> 00:10:45.300
in the most medically sound way possible.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:50.300
So you are, I mean,
heroes to keep doing that

00:10:50.317 --> 00:10:52.530
and thank you for what you do.

00:10:52.530 --> 00:10:53.363
- Thank you.

00:10:53.363 --> 00:10:54.400
I appreciate you saying that.

